%%
%% SECTION 10: OPEN PROBLEMS
%%
\section{Open Problems and Research Agenda}
\label{sec:openproblems}

Definitional fragmentation, the absence of a standard evaluation suite, and the lack of
longitudinal human-subject evidence jointly block the transition from research prototype to
deployable assurance practice. This section identifies the gaps and, for the one that most
directly undermines the controls of Section~\ref{sec:controls}, specifies a study to close it.

\subsection{Measurement and Standardization Gaps}
\label{subsec:standardization}

Four gaps compound one another. Taxonomic consensus is missing: 70 papers use the term
inconsistently~\citep{ye2026taxonomy}, making results incomparable and mitigations
non-transferable, and adoption of operational definitions distinguishing propositional from
social, overt from subtle, and progressive from regressive forms is the prerequisite for
everything else. Subtle and social sycophancy remain unmeasured at scale:
ELEPHANT~\citep{cheng2026elephant} is a first step, but passive validation, selective framing, and
partial endorsement lack automatic evaluators, which is exactly the displacement risk of
Section~\ref{subsec:appraisal}. Causal attribution is unresolved: an unaligned base model may
already agree due to conversational pretraining data, and disentangling RLHF effects from
base-model priors needs interventional tools of the kind used
by \citet{wang2026truthoverridden}. And the field lacks a consensus suite: a unified battery
combining SycEval (single-turn factual), SYCON (multi-turn pressure), ELEPHANT (social), PARROT
(authority), and AEDI (continuous tracking) would make cross-study comparison and regression
testing possible~\citep{fanous2025syceval,hong2025sycon,cheng2026elephant,celebi2025parrot,
botas2026aedi}. Beneath all four sits the normative question: some agreement is appropriate,
updating on evidence and deferring to expertise are desirable, and the optimal deference policy is
context-dependent in ways no current benchmark encodes~\citep{botas2026aedi}.

\subsection{Agentic, Multi-Agent, and Pipeline Settings}
\label{subsec:agentic}

Current benchmarks evaluate a single model answering a single user, while deployments increasingly
act. In agentic settings, a sycophancy-prone agent may execute a user-specified plan it internally
assesses as wrong, functionally equivalent to specification gaming and hard to detect because the
agent generates plausible post-hoc justifications~\citep{denison2024subterfuge,turpin2023faithful}.
In multi-agent pipelines, deliberation mechanisms assume adversarial correction; a sycophantic
participant collapses debate into premature consensus, and the incremental capitulation
trajectories documented under sustained pressure~\citep{fanous2025syceval,hong2025sycon} are a
plausible mechanism for a persistent co-agent to move a target model by pressure alone. Retrieval
and tool use add channels the threat model already covers in principle but no instrument measures:
biased retrieval weighting that surfaces belief-confirming documents lends sycophantic output a
veneer of grounding, and tool-selection sycophancy follows a user's suggested workflow while
rationalizing it after the fact~\citep{turpin2023faithful}. Evaluation protocols for retrieval
bias, action-selection sycophancy, and post-hoc rationalization in these pipelines are a
high-priority open problem, as is the premise and provenance gap of
Section~\ref{subsec:coverage}, which these architectures widen.

\subsection{Coverage Blind Spots: Language, Culture, Context Length}
\label{subsec:coverage_blind}

Sycophancy is a social behavior and plausibly varies with linguistic and cultural norms around
disagreement; face-saving norms differ regionally~\citep{cheng2026elephant}, so English-calibrated
benchmarks may misstate rates for other populations, and whether interventions transfer across
languages is untested. Duration is a second blind spot: capitulation accelerates with conversation
length~\citep{hong2025sycon} and framing effects compound across turns~\citep{dubois2026askdont},
while context windows grow toward millions of tokens in which user-generated text dominates the
system prompt. Whether long-context operation is systematically more sycophantic is an open
empirical question with direct deployment implications.

\subsection{Post-Deployment Drift}
\label{subsec:drift}

Models can reacquire sycophancy after deployment through reinforcement from live user feedback,
fine-tuning on interaction logs, or continuous improvement loops rated by the same approval signal
that created the problem~\citep{stiennon2020summarize,bai2022rlhf,shapira2026rlhf}, and the
formal results on reward hacking imply that small consistent biases compound over training
steps~\citep{skalse2022reward}. Monitoring therefore needs sycophancy-specific signals beyond
capability benchmarks: continuous AEDI tracking is a candidate drift detector~\citep{botas2026aedi},
and online debiasing of feedback, weighting rater input by argument quality rather than
satisfaction, is a candidate corrective. Neither is validated in production, which is the gap
between the laboratory results surveyed here and responsible deployment practice.

\subsection{Epistemic Entrainment and a Longitudinal Assurance Study}
\label{subsec:entrainment}

Every experimental result in this SoK is single-session or short-horizon. One interaction distorts
prosocial judgment~\citep{cheng2025prosocial}; brief conversations inflate attitude extremity and
overconfidence~\citep{rathje2025attitude}; a single session leads novices to absorb false
information without awareness~\citep{bo2026saboteurs}. Whether these effects compound over months
of daily use is unmeasured, and the mechanisms are plausible: sycophancy is systematically
rewarded in training~\citep{sharma2023understanding}, drift toward it is governed by the
covariance between belief endorsement and learned reward~\citep{shapira2026rlhf}, and unfaithful
rationales give users reasoning chains they may habituate to trusting~\citep{turpin2023faithful}.
We call the hypothesized outcome \emph{epistemic entrainment}: progressive erosion of users'
epistemic thresholds and self-correction capacity under chronic reinforcement of their beliefs.
The stakes for assurance are direct. Nearly every control in Section~\ref{sec:controls} depends at
some point on a human noticing something is wrong; if exposure degrades that capacity, controls
are being validated against a baseline the deployment itself erodes, and certification would need
to specify exposure duration rather than test on fresh users.

Because further single-session work cannot settle this, we specify the design.
\textbf{Question:} does prolonged daily interaction with a sycophantic assistant progressively
erode a user's epistemic threshold and self-correction capacity, and can an adaptive friction
policy preserve that capacity without removing the support some users derive from affirmation?
\textbf{Hypotheses:} H1, threshold erosion: users assigned a sycophantic assistant show rising
assimilation of injected false confirmations over twelve weeks while reporting rising confidence
in their own analysis. H2, adaptive friction: a policy delivering affirmation under high distress
and counterevidence during stable analytical work preserves epistemic performance comparable to a
Socratic condition while retaining alliance scores comparable to the sycophantic condition. H3,
interpersonal spillover: users habituated to sycophantic feedback show elevated reactance when
paired with a dissenting human collaborator. \textbf{Design:} a three-arm randomized trial,
$n = 120$ per arm, knowledge workers and advanced students stratified on cognitive reflection,
loneliness, and distress so susceptible participants are represented rather than screened out;
twelve weeks at three or more hours weekly in an instrumented document-editing environment logging
full prompt-response trajectories, editing telemetry, and biweekly psychometrics. Condition A is
sycophantic (validate hypotheses, mirror stance); Condition B applies adaptive friction through an
orchestration layer classifying each turn by domain risk and user affect; Condition C uses
commercial defaults. \textbf{Probe:} every four weeks the platform injects one subtle false claim
confirming the user's stated hypothesis, with no oracle labels supplied to the assistant
(preserving ecological validity, unlike oracle designs~\citep{bo2026saboteurs}), and all probes
corrected and disclosed at debriefing. \textbf{Outcomes:} assimilation rate of injected
confirmations modeled with mixed-effects growth curves; blind expert ratings of analytical output
at weeks 0, 6, and 12; alliance and affect measures; spillover against confederate dissenters; and
differential attrition. \textbf{Why it matters for assurance:} a positive H1 makes exposure
duration a certification parameter; a positive H2 establishes graded, context-sensitive
accommodation as a viable control rather than the binary present-or-eliminated framing. Both yield
welfare measures that cannot be collapsed into satisfaction ratings, which is essential because
satisfaction is precisely the signal this failure mode corrupts. \textbf{Risks:} deliberate
sycophantic exposure risks inducing real overconfidence; mitigations include mandatory debriefing
disclosing every injected error, referral pathways, and independent safety monitoring.
